# Searching by name
find /etc -name "nginx.conf"
Find exact filename match. Case-SENSITIVE! nginx.conf ≠ Nginx.conf. Full name must match.

find /etc -iname "nginx.conf"
Case-INSENSITIVE match. -i prefix = ignore case. Matches nginx.conf, Nginx.conf, NGINX.CONF all at once!

find /var/log -name "*.log"
Wildcard: * = any characters. Find ALL .log files. ALWAYS quote the pattern — prevents shell expansion!

find . -name "app?.log"
? = exactly ONE character wildcard. Matches app1.log, app2.log, appA.log. NOT app10.log (two chars).

find . -name "app[123].log"
[ ] = character class. Matches app1.log, app2.log, app3.log ONLY. Like regex character class!

find . -not -name "*.log"
NOT operator — exclude files matching pattern. Find everything EXCEPT .log files. Same as: find . ! -name "*.log"

find / -path "*/nginx/*.conf"
-path matches FULL PATH including directories. Find .conf files inside any nginx directory anywhere. More powerful than -name!

find . -regex ".*\.\(log\|txt\)$"
Use REGEX pattern for matching! -regex matches full path. -iregex = case insensitive. More powerful than -name wildcards!

# Searching by type
-type f
Regular FILES only. Most common! Excludes directories, symlinks, devices. Always specify -type f when searching for files!
find /var -type f -name "*.log"

-type d
DIRECTORIES only. Find all directories matching criteria. Great for setting directory permissions separately from files.

find /var/www -type d

-type l
SYMBOLIC LINKS only. Find all symlinks. Combine with -xtype l to find BROKEN symlinks (target doesn't exist)!
find /etc -type l

-xtype l
BROKEN symlinks — type of what is pointed to. If target gone, xtype = l (symlink). Essential for finding dangling symlinks!
find /etc -xtype l → broken symlinks

-type b / -type c
Block/character DEVICE files. Disks (/dev/sda), terminals (/dev/tty). Useful for security audits — devices outside /dev are suspicious!

find / -type b -not -path "/dev/*"
-type p / -type s
Named PIPES (p) and SOCKETS (s). Processes communicate through these. Docker and apps create socket files.
find /tmp -type s → socket files
